A||N||9||Seanh||Possible Complete Rebuilding of 86||||5||
Z||000000||Seanh||09-24-2000||07:34 PM||||I have been thinking about completely rebuilding my 86GT this Dec-Feb. I was wonding how long to expect this to take? I plan on removing all body panels, and disassembling the car, removing all rust and generally cleaning the car up. Replacing all those small parts the might show signs of wear and tear and a few more upgrades I have in mind. I still am not sure what to do about the engine in this car. I have pretty much decided not to do the N* in this car, at this time. I would like to keep the engine stock for the most part. It has 118K on this and is running good with the exception of the clogged cat. So an engine rebuilding is not necessary, but I will have the engine out of the car, so I might as well do it now. I was wondering two things about the engine: 1. Should I use a rebuild kit, like the one at the Fiero Store? 2. Is the Fiero store's Performance rebuild kit worth the extra $$$ over the stock rebuild kit? And lastly, should I look into building up to a 3.1? Or will I notice a big difference in power? How much will I expect to pay to build up to a 3.1 if I do so?  <P>Well, I think thats about all I can think of asking right now. My main concern is how long it will take with or without rebuilding the engine. For thoes of you who dont know me, I do go to school till 2:00, and I work after school, but I might not be working there after X-mas. I would say I have average machinal skills. I have done some engine swaps with some friends on a 70 Chevy, but I think this Fiero might be a little more difficult. Thanks for the help.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||09-24-2000||08:06 PM||||While I can't help you with the engine info you want, I have dissassembled a Fiero.  A couple of years ago I took my 84 apart for about the same reasons you want to.  I didn't dissassemble the engine at that time, but everything else was removed including wiring and brake lines and such until the chassis was totally stripped except for the rear window and decklid hinges.  As I recall it took about 24 to 30 hours to take the car apart, but I had done portions at one time or another over the years, so it will probably take somewhat longer for a first timer.  I spent several weeks doing parts cleanup and replacement.  I also repainted the body panels.  It took about 50 hours to reassemble everything.  I made lots of notes during dissassembly and they were a big help, as was having a factory shop manual.  I wish I had had my 22P book then, because it has excellent illustrations.<BR>Have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||6
Z||000002||Seanh||09-24-2000||09:08 PM||||I was wondering if you or anyone else has some information on the process. I mean like what order to disassemble the car in, and to reassemble it in. I have a Haynes manual but not a shop manual. Where can I get a shop manual for it, and how much are they? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||09-24-2000||10:17 PM||||I just noticed the Fiero Store has factory shop manuals for $97  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">. Think I should get one? Does it give more detail into disassembling the entire car? I am still concerned about the order of doing things though. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||2xsess||09-24-2000||10:22 PM||||Well, it's pretty easy to remove the body panels. I suggest you start with the hood and decklid, then it will easier to get to all the bolts on the fenders and front and rear bumper covers, After the hood and Decklid remove the rocker panels, this is necessary to get to the lower bolts on the fenders and quarter panels. Also you'll have to drop the headliner to get to the bolts that hold on the rear clip. I never could figure out how to remove the roof section though.<P>long but hopefully helpful post! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck and keep us posted <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||09-24-2000||10:24 PM||||Unfortunatly, I didn't save my list I made of what order I took stuff off the car. As well as I can remember, it was something like this.<BR>I first removed the interior trim (panels behind the doors, along the floor, upper trim and headliner), and then all of the outer body panels.  Exact order isn't important, but the rear roof section should be left for last since it was the first panel installed at the factory.  Next was the rear suspension and dropping the cradle with the engine.  After that I started at the rear of the car and removed all the hoses, tubes and wire harness from the engine bay.  Next I removed the front suspension parts.  I wanted to get the coil springs out of the front before I took too much weight out of the front of the car. If the car is too light you may have a hard time jacking up the lower control arms to unload the pivots.  After that I removed all the parts out of the front compartment except for the heater/ac unit.  Exact order is up to you. Just start with whatever is easiest to get to and go down through the layers.  Next was the interior.  Again, just start with the easiest stuff and go from there.  It's easier to get to the door hinge bolts and electrical plugs after all the interior is out of the car.  Next, I went under the car and removed the gas tank, a/c lines, heater lines, radiator tubes, and brake and clutch hydraulic lines.  One of the last things I took out was the heater assembly and the windshield wiper linkage.  The heater unbolts and pulls out through the front compartment in one unit.  I know this is far from a complete list, but this general order worked for me.<BR>Shop manuals might be available from Helm, Inc. or on E-bay.  If you can find a 22P parts book (E-bay) it would be a big help as it shows virtually every part and how it goes together.  Shop manuals are probably $60-$100, 22P books around $50-$70.<BR>The most important thing is to make good notes and maybe take pictures.  Have Fun! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||09-25-2000||05:44 PM||||Okay, I will go ahead and get those two books. I would also like to know: Do you guys think I should rebuild my engine? It has 118K on it, and runs good, I think my only problem is a clogged cat. When I start the car, alot of white smoke pores from the exahust pipes. It is also, (just filled up today) now getting 10-11 MPG City, I do have a lead foot and I drive like theres no tomarrow, but shouldnt I still be getting better milage? What effects does eliminating the cat have? Will I loose any performance? I could get by with not having a cat. here. As we usally never have any inspections and when we do they just come out and see that it is really a Poniac Fiero and looks close to a 1986 and is the same color. Never more then that.<P>I will start posting a thread on here when I start this project and keep you up-to-date on what I get done.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-25-2000||08:01 PM||||Some of it's hard to say.... I don't know how much rust you have......<P>Unless there's some real problem with the roof or the windshield flanges I'd leave the roof on. It's too easy to break it.<P>You can take everyting else off in whatever order you want once the deck lid and hood are off. some things like the rear panels will force you to take them off in a certain order. You'll likely see it as you work. <P>You'll want the front fenders off first if you are taking off the doors complete. The bolts are a pain to get at otherwise. If you are just skining the doors it doesn't matter. You may want to read my cave article on doors and hinges to help decide what to do with them. (If you are going to skin the doors it's easier to do with them on the car. Take the rest of the door off after that if you want.)<P>As for the motor there's no reason at all to rebuild it unless something is wrong with it. You say it runs good. If it's not smoking or showing goofy oil pressure...... Do a compression check and see what you get. That may help you decide.<P>If it's a stick you may want to drop the cradle now just to do the clutch. Otherwise I'd leave the whole cradle alone with the exception of changing bushings. You can do that w/o dropping the whole thing.<P>Like I said I don't know how much rust you have.... ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||09-25-2000||09:39 PM||||IMO the rust is pretty bad. It covers alot of the frame of the car. I know that its not really a big deal, as this car will be fine for years to come without any rust problems. I am going to do this mainly to replace things and to gain more knowlege about the car. I usally dont drive it in winter anyway. I would like to restore the car to mint condition and to see if I might have any problems. I will do a compression check to see, but I think the engine is in good order. I am still wondering about removing the cat and either replacing it with a high flow one, or eliminating it all together. I am planning on stripping the entire interor and replacing carpeting and some broken trim panels. My car is an automatic. Do you think I should pull the engine even if I am not going to rebuild it? I am planning on going to some national fiero shows and my engine needs to be cleaned up. It could use some new paint too. I have a quick question. Can you paint your exahust manifolds with heat resistant paint? Or should I bite the bullet and buy some new Jet coated ones? Would it be to difficult to drop the engine and tranny since I will have the car apart already?<P>Thanks<P>BTW, the engine is a V6. and I plan on taking to doors off complete, and redoing the internals off the car.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 09-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||prof bobo||09-26-2000||04:29 PM||||I'm in the middle of what you are proposing to start.<P>I removed deck/hood lids just to make everything else easier to get to, then removed body panels working my way from front to back.  I have shop/Haynes/22p manuals, which can offer 'clues' when it isn't obvious how something is put together, and Haynes seems to be geared more towards D.I.Y-er's who don't have all of the nifty special-purpose tools like a GM shop does.<BR>I disassembled the front-end 2 weekends ago, did most of the rear last weekend plus most of the top of the engine connections.  The engine comes out tonight.<P>I have holes rusted through the windshield flanges, spot rust on frame rails over rear wheelwells.  I figure if I can get the rust off to clean bare metal, I can evaluate whether or not just paint is enough or patching will be required.  It strikes me that the nice thing about the Fiero frame (at least in the areas I have to deal with) is that you don't have to be very pretty with repairs. I expect I'll be able to get away with just bending some sheet metal to fit over the bad area and then just overwelding it -the body panels will hide the 'scars'!<P>Plus I'd been having weird electrical problems -I'm gonna unwrap, test and redo the harnesses with heat-shrink tubing.<P>The engine was rebuilt once, but I'm probably going to do it again and add some minor performance mods.  Since a good portion of the work is getting it out and in anyway, I think it always pays to go the rest of the way.  It's too easy to say "I wish I'd . . ." after its all bolted back in and having regrets for not having done what you could have when you had the chance.<P>Anyway, good luck!||||reg||1
